\chapter{Preprint fallback}
\label{preprint-module}

Enrichment (\cref{enrich-module}) fills a paper's [[pdf_url]] from the
search providers and Unpaywall (\cref{unpaywall-module}), but for a
paywalled paper without any registered open-access copy both come up
empty.  Very often, though, the work \emph{is} readable---as a preprint
on arXiv, HAL, SSRN, bioRxiv, or another server.  The preprint is a
different record with a different DOI, so no per-DOI lookup on the
published paper will ever surface it by itself.

This chapter closes that gap: given a paper whose PDF could not be
found, locate a preprint version of the same work and \emph{adopt} it.
Adoption replaces the entry wholesale---DOI, venue, URL, and PDF---
rather than just borrowing the preprint's PDF link.  This is a
citation-integrity decision: whoever reads the preprint should cite the
preprint, not the published version they never saw.  The published
DOI is preserved in the [[published_versions]] field
(\cref{sec:version-merge}) so the substitution stays auditable and the
published version is recognized as already-present when a search is
re-run.

\section{Design overview}

The central difficulty is linking two records that share almost no
metadata: a preprint and its published version differ in DOI, venue,
URL, and often year.  The only data that reliably maps between them is
the title and the authors.  We therefore use two kinds of links, in
order of trustworthiness:

\begin{description}
\item[Authoritative DOI links] Some links are asserted by the
  publishers and indexes themselves: OpenAlex merges a preprint deposit
  into the published work's [[locations]] array, and Crossref's
  [[has-preprint]] relation points from the published DOI to the
  preprint DOI (\cref{crossref-module}).  These need no matching at
  all and run on every enrichment---each is a single, cached lookup.
\item[Title+author search] When no authoritative link exists, we can
  search the preprint servers for the title and match candidates on
  the strict identity rule [[legacy_hash_paper_id]]
  (\cref{utils-module}): exact normalized title plus first author's
  surname.  A looser, fuzzy match would find more preprints but could
  silently adopt the \emph{wrong} paper---fatal in a systematic
  review---so we reuse the exact rule the codebase already trusts for
  DOI-less identity.  Because even an exact match carries residual
  risk (and costs extra requests per paper), this tier only runs when
  the caller opts in ([[--preprint-search]] on the CLI).
\end{description}

Whatever the tier, a candidate is only adopted if it actually yields a
PDF link: swapping the entry's identity without gaining a readable
copy would be pure churn.  For the same reason [[find_preprint]]
returns [[None]] when the paper already has a PDF.

The module is pure orchestration.  All network access and caching
lives in the clients it composes---the OpenAlex provider
(\cref{sec:openalex}), the Crossref client, and
Unpaywall---so results here are cheap to recompute from their caches
and this module needs no cache of its own.  It follows the same
silent-failure contract as the providers: any failing tier logs and
falls through to the next, and [[find_preprint]] never raises.

